Verdict: MAJOR

## Findings
- [major] Approval-gate drift is still not mitigated strongly enough because cleanup of the real gates is conditional on a future rerun outcome instead of required immediately. The plan’s own `Status` says the live GitHub `status:plan-approved` label and local `.planning/plan-approved/2460.md` marker “reflect older approval-state drift, not approval of this current draft revision,” but `implement_with_tdd()` only requires cleanup “if fresh external review still returns MAJOR,” and the matching Acceptance Criterion repeats that same conditional. That leaves a known-false approved state live in the real gates for the current draft lineage, which is the exact governance hazard the plan claims to address.
- [major] The stale review-state contradiction is not fully gone. The top metadata says `Status: draft (fresh 2026-04-23 external reruns still returned MAJOR...)`, while the later `Adversarial Review Summary` says `Current draft state: pending fresh rerun.` Those statements do not cleanly describe the same revision state. If this draft is still pending a fresh rerun, the header cannot also present fresh reruns as already having returned MAJOR for this draft lineage.
- [minor] The approval-drift evidence is still not revision-bound in an operational way. The plan says the live label and local marker belong to an “older approval-state drift” and allows “remove, supersede, or revision-bind” as remedies, but nowhere defines a deterministic revision identity for the plan (for example exact artifact set, content hash, or explicit revision id). Without that, “older revision” remains narrative, not an enforceable rule.

## Checks performed
- Checked the supplied plan text’s `Status`, `Evidence`, `Pseudocode`, `Files to Change`, `Acceptance Criteria`, and `Adversarial Review Summary` for internal consistency.
- Checked whether approval-drift handling now targets the real gates named in the plan: live GitHub `status:plan-approved` and local `.planning/plan-approved/2460.md`.
- Checked whether the mitigation is operationally strong enough to prevent stale approval reuse before any further posting or execution.
- Checked whether the stale review-state narrative contradiction was actually removed rather than moved to another section.
- Checked whether any remaining approval-blocking defect still exists after the stated r10 changes.
